= Compiler — Technical =

== Pipeline ==
A common architecture is:

<pre>
source
→ tokens
→ syntax tree
→ typed/semantic IR
→ optimized IR
→ target code
</pre>

Actual compilers can interleave or repeat these stages.

== Parsing ==
Lexers recognize token structure; parsers recognize grammatical structure.

Context-free grammars are common for syntax, while semantic constraints such as types and declarations require additional analysis.

== Intermediate representations ==
IRs make control flow, data flow, types, memory effects or machine operations explicit at different abstraction levels.

SSA form is a widely used compiler IR discipline.

== Optimization ==
Optimizations require semantic assumptions.

Examples include constant propagation, dead-code elimination, inlining, loop transformations, vectorization and register allocation.

An optimization is correct only if it preserves permitted observable behavior.

== Correctness ==
Verified compilation can prove semantic preservation between source and target under formal language semantics.

Testing remains important because full compiler stacks and toolchains exceed the scope of many proofs.

